{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target proposes a strict cascade of progressively stronger causal-decorrelation tests, yet never identifies the causal mechanism that forces each level to depend on the prior: it asserts that higher-level checks 'verify' lower-level signals, but provides no mechanism explaining why a decorrelated signal lacking refutation capacity would fail, or why perturbation sensitivity without population statistics is causally insufficient. Without such a mechanism, the levels could be independent evidence streams rather than a cascade, which makes the proposed integration relation (cascade/dependence) unsupported.","target":"1e6ffb00220c4ccb19c95598920bd733e0cbad74b4629c129a5bdc27e7d977f2"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims the isolated artifact 'shares mechanism' with SRC_003 via causal sensitivity to falsification, but the two mechanisms differ in their causal structure in a way that undermines the shared-mechanism relation: SRC_003's mechanism is population-level statistical power (variance reduction over many trials), whereas the isolated artifact's mechanism is within-trail causal-path coverage. These do not share a causal mechanism; they share only a superficial goal (sensitivity to falsification). The causal drivers are distinct — one is a sampling mechanism, the other a graph-tracing mechanism — so the asserted relation of a shared mechanism is not substantiated, only a re-description of the endpoints under a common label.","target":"5dd528eb6e7c2b897d80c91b72df115e5812a87f360d149f2c28fcde528e9302"}]}